{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788211196,"nonce":"xLGwOTFMT6O+tvl0fMWgQB8hPpo=","title":"No observer surfaces the quest log, so the agent can only answer quests it was told about by a letter","message":"Wave 2 — eyes. Depends: 2.4 (the observer family this joins). Feeds 3.5, M3.\nFiled by the orchestrator, 2026-08-31, at Evan's prompting (\"how do you plan to\ndo quests too?\").\n\nTHE GAP\n\n3.5's recon settled how to ACCEPT a quest, and it is not what the spec assumed:\na quest cannot be accepted from its letter. `NewQuestLetter.OpenLetter` switches\nto the Quests tab, selects the quest and REMOVES the letter; its `Choices` are\nonly View / Jump / Close. So accept is its own verb —\n`QuestPart_Choice.Choose(choice)` then `Quest.Accept(pawn)`, both public, gated by\n`QuestUtility.CanAcceptQuest` and per-pawn `CanPawnAcceptQuest`.\n\nBut **nothing surfaces the quest log itself.** `interactions` (2.4) reports open\nletters and every window on the stack — that is \"what is awaiting input\", not\n\"what quests exist\". So today the agent can accept a quest it happened to be told\nabout while the letter was open, and has no way to answer:\n\n- what quests are currently available, and which are already accepted\n- what each one DEMANDS (a pawn? an item? a time window?)\n- what it PAYS, and whether a reward choice is outstanding\n- when it EXPIRES\n\nA quest whose letter arrived during an advance the agent did not read closely is,\ntoday, invisible forever.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC\n\n**M3 rides on it.** 5.2 is the Factions/Guests suite, and Hospitality/Guests\ncontent arrives largely as quests. A suite that cannot enumerate quests cannot\nassert on them.\n\n**And there is a trap that makes the gap dangerous rather than merely\ninconvenient:** accepting a quest while two or more reward choices are\noutstanding LOGS A RED ERROR and auto-picks the first. Since a red error breaches\nthis project's standing zero-red-errors invariant, the accept verb MUST choose\nbefore accepting — and it cannot choose what it cannot read. So 3.5's accept verb\nis only safe if this read exists.\n\nSCOPE\n\n- A `quests` verb in the 2.4 observer family: list quests with state (available /\n  accepted / expired / dismissed), demands, rewards, outstanding choices, and\n  ticks-until-expiry.\n- A drill-down for one quest, in the sections shape 2.2/2.4 already use.\n- `dismissed` reported as what it is — cosmetic filtering, NOT a decline. An agent\n  that reads `dismissed` as \"we said no\" will be wrong.\n\nHAZARD NOTE\n\nTwo of the letter-side accessors are already known-dangerous and this verb must\nnot repeat them. `ChoiceLetter.Choices` is an ITERATOR that constructs\n`DiaOption`s, and `ChoiceLetter.Option_ViewInQuestsTab` dereferences `quest.name`\nBEFORE its own null check. Separately `LetterStack.BundleLetter`'s getter burns a\nscribed letter ID via `LetterMaker.MakeLetter` -\u003e `GetNextLetterID`. Check every\nquest accessor for the same shape before adopting it, per `WorldSafe` discipline;\n`QuestPart` subclasses are third-party-extensible, so a modded part's getter is\narbitrary code.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- On a colony with at least one available and one accepted quest, `quests` lists\n  both with correct state, demands, rewards and expiry.\n- A quest with an outstanding `QuestPart_Choice` reports the choice as outstanding\n  and enumerates the options — the read 3.5's accept verb needs in order to choose\n  before accepting.\n- `dismissed` is reported distinctly from declined/expired.\n- Reading the quest log twice leaves the save byte-identical over the quest\n  region — the same double-read proof 2.4 used for research progress.\n- Zero red errors.","files":null}]}